#problem()[
  Let $R$ be a noetherian ring. We have shown (or will show) that $X = "Spec"(R)$ can be written as the union of finitely many irreducible closed subsets, $X = X_1 union X_2 union dots.c union X_m$, such that $X_i$ is not contained in $X_j$ for any $i != j$. Show that such a decomposition of $X$ is unique up to reordering the $X_i$'s. It is called the _decomposition of $X$ into its irreducible components_.
]
#solution[
  Assume the contrary, $A_1 union ... union A_a$ and $B_1 union ... union B_b$ are two decompositions of $X$ into irreducible. Note the following that $ A_i = union.big_(j = 1)^(b) A_i inter B_j $ and that $A_i inter B_j$ is closed. Assume the contrary that $S = {j in [b] : A_i inter B_j != emptyset}$ doesn't have only 1 element. $abs(S) != 0 => A_i = emptyset$ which is not true. So $abs(S) > 1$. Thus $ A_i & = union.big_(j in S) A_i inter B_j, $ but this contradicts the assumption that $A_i$ is irreducible. Thus by contradiction, $exists j_i in [b]$ such that $A_i subset.eq B_(j_i)$. Let $phi: [a] -> [b]$ where $i -> j_i$.

  By a similar argument, there is a $psi: [b] -> [a]$ where $j -> i_j$ such that $B_j subset.eq A_(j_i).$

  Assume the contrary, that $psi(phi(i)) = k != i$. Thus $A_i subset.eq B_(phi(i)) subset.eq A_(k)$. This contradicts the maximality of $A_i$, meaning $psi compose phi = 1_([a])$. By a symmetric argument, $phi compose psi = 1_[b].$ Moreover this tells us that $ A_i subset.eq B_(phi(i)) subset.eq A_i subset.eq B_(phi(i)) => A_i = B_(phi(i)). $ meaning $phi$ is just a permutation.
]

#problem()[
  Write $AA^3_CC$ for affine 3-space over $CC$, meaning $"Spec" CC[x, y, z]$. Let $X$ be the closed subset ${x^2 = y z, x z = z} subset AA^3_CC$. (As always, this means the subset where both equations hold.) Decompose $X$ into its irreducible components.
]
#solution[
  $
    X & = {x^2 - y z = 0, x z - z = 0} \
      & = {x^2 - y z = 0, z(x - 1) = 0} \
      & = {x^2 - y z = 0, z = 0} union {x^2 - y z = 0, x - 1 = 0} \
      & = {x^2 = 0, z = 0} union {y z - 1 = 0, x - 1 = 0} \
      & = {x^2 = 0, z = 0} union {y z = 1, x = 1} \
      & = {x = 0, z = 0} union {y z = 1, x = 1}
  $

  Consider $ CC[x, y, z] -> CC[y] \ 1 -> 1 quad x -> 0 quad y -> y quad z -> z, $ the kernel of such a map is $(x, z)$, which therefore is prime. $cl({(x, z)}) = {x = 0, z = 0}$. The closure of a single point is always irreducible because a single point is always irreducible.

  Consider $ CC[x, y, z] ->> (CC[y, z]) / ((y z - 1)) iso (CC[z])[1/z] iso CC[z, z^(-1)] \ 1 -> 1 quad x -> 1 quad y -> 1/z quad z -> z $

  The kernel of this map is $(x - 1, y z - 1)$ and since the image is a domain, the ideal is also prime. Thus $cl({(x - 1, y z - 1)}) = {x = 1, y z = 1}$ and by the same argument above it is irreducible.

  Furthemore suppose a ideal $I$ contains $(x, z) union (x - 1, y z - 1)$ or in other words contains, $(x, z, x - 1, y z - 1) => 1 in I$ because $-(x - 1) + x in I$. Thus $I = C[x, y, z]$ and thus cannot be prime. Thus no prime ideal contains both $(x - 1, y z - 1)$ and $(x, z)$, and thus ${x = 0, z = 0} inter {y z = 1, x = 1} = emptyset$.
]

#problem()[
  Let $k$ be a field. Let $R$ be the $k$-subalgebra of $k[x, y]$ generated by $y, x y, x^2 y, dots$. Show that $R$ is not finitely generated as a $k$-algebra. Is $R$ noetherian? Also, describe in geometric terms (thinking of polynomials as functions) which polynomials are in the subring $R$.
]
#solution[
  Note that $ R & = k oplus (oplus.big_(a >= 0 \ b >= 1)^(oo) k x^a y^b ) $ as a vectorspace. Assume the contrary, that $R = k[f_1, ..., f_k]$ where $f_1, ..., f_k in k[x, y]$, ie every element of $R$ can be written as a $k$ linear combination of monomials of the form $f_(i_1) ... f_(i_m)$. Without loss of generality, $f_1, ..., f_k$ have no constant term as $ k[f_1, ..., f_k] = k[f_1 - f_1 (0,0), ..., f_k - f_k (0,0)] $. At least 1 polynomial, has a nonzero coefficient in front of a term with y degree equal to 1. Let $N_i$ equal the degree of $x$ when the degree of $y$ equals to $1$ in $f_i$. In other words,
  $ f_i (x, y) & = sum_(k = 1)^(N_i) a_i x^k y + ("Higher order terms of" y) $

  Let $N = max(N_1, ..., N_k)$. You cannot get $x^(N + 1) y$ as the $k$-linear combination of $f_1, ..., f_k$. Furthermore, any product of $f_1, ..., f_k$ will have minimum $y$ degree greater than 1. Hence $x^(N + 1) y$ cannot be reached by $k[f_1, ..., f_k]$. Thus a contradiction occurs.

  $R$ is not noetherian, because the ideal $I = (y, x y, x^2 y, ...)$ is not finitely generated, ie the ideal of polynomials in $R$ with no constant term. This is not finitely generated for the same reason $R$ is not finitely generated, $x^a y in.not (f_1, ..., f_k)$ for $f_i in I$ for $a$ large enough.


  The polynomials in $R$ are the polynomials which are constant at ${y = 0}$.
]
